  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1027 PM MDT Thu Jul 30 2026\n\nModels are in good agreement with the high now to our south over New \nMexico continuing to build west into the Desert Southwest tomorrow \ninto Saturday where it is looking to drop anchor for the next week \nand shut off the tap to the deep moisture. That being said look for \nsome moisture to work its way up under the high into the southern \nmountains to keep isolated afternoon showers and thunderstorms in \nthe forecast through the weekend becoming more scattered by mid next \nweek. In the short term, residual moisture in the area tomorrow \nafternoon will likely spawn a few isolated showers and thunderstorms \nover the mountains, especially along the Divide. \n\nAs the high builds in to the southwest and the atmosphere dries out \nover eastern Utah and Western Colorado, look for temperatures to \nclimb from about five degrees above normal tomorrow to ten to 15 \ndegrees above normal by early next week. With this heat, we've \nissued heat advisories and warnings for the lower valleys through \nthe weekend. If you'll be out this weekend, be sure to drink plenty \nof liquids, take breaks in shaded areas and avoid the hottest part \nof the day between 10 AM and 4 PM. These hot temperatures and dry \nair will drive the relative humidity down into the mid to low teens, \nand with afternoon winds gusting 25 mph in some areas, we'll see \nlocalized areas of critical fire weather conditions across the \nregion. It's just that time of the year when if it's not \nthunderstorms and possible flash flooding, it's hot and dry fire \nweather. Stay tuned for updates.",
